The three rows in @tiago-peixoto's table do not have the same cause, and separating them narrows where to look. Reading the code at tag `rust-v0.153.4`, the version measured:

**Row 1 is what the code does by design.** `background_terminal_max_timeout` is not consulted on the `exec_command` path at all. `exec_command` puts its requested yield through `clamp_yield_time()`:

```rust
// codex-rs/core/src/unified_exec/mod.rs
pub(crate) const MIN_YIELD_TIME_MS: u64 = 250;
pub(crate) const MAX_YIELD_TIME_MS: u64 = 30_000;

pub(crate) fn clamp_yield_time(yield_time_ms: u64) -> u64 { ...
    yield_time_ms.clamp(MIN_YIELD_TIME_MS, MAX_YIELD_TIME_MS)
}
```

called from `process_manager.rs::exec_command`. So the first yield of any background command is capped at 30s regardless of what is requested or configured, which matches the measured 30.002s exactly. That reads as a documentation mismatch rather than a bug: the config reference describes `background_terminal_max_timeout` without noting that it does not govern `exec_command`.

**Row 2 is a different thing.** On the empty-`write_stdin` path the same tag does honour the setting:

```rust
// codex-rs/core/src/unified_exec/process_manager.rs
let time_ms = request.yield_time_ms.max(MIN_YIELD_TIME_MS);
if request.input.is_empty() {
    time_ms.clamp(MIN_EMPTY_YIELD_TIME_MS, self.max_write_stdin_yield_time_ms)
} else {
    time_ms.min(MAX_YIELD_TIME_MS)
}
```

`max_write_stdin_yield_time_ms` is set once per session in `session.rs` from `UnifiedExecProcessManager::new(config.background_terminal_max_timeout)`, and the `write_stdin` handler passes `chars` straight through as `input`. With the documented 300,000ms default and a 290,000ms request, that expression evaluates to 290,000. The measured 31.0s is instead within a second of `MAX_YIELD_TIME_MS`, which is the ceiling on the *other* branch. So on that build either the empty branch was not taken or the manager was constructed with a smaller bound than the documented default.

That distinction matters for the question being asked here, because row 2 decides whether the empty-poll case needs a new mechanism or is a wiring problem in an existing one. A cheap way to tell them apart without patching anything: run the same empty poll on a command that produces no output, and check whether the ~30s ceiling holds when `background_terminal_max_timeout` is set explicitly in `config.toml` rather than left to the default. If it still returns at ~30s with an explicit value, the configured bound is not reaching the process manager.

Row 1 is separable from the larger design discussion and cheap either way: `exec_command` could take the same bound as the empty-poll branch instead of the fixed 30s cap, or the config reference could state the current limit. Today the first yield of every background start costs a model turn that no configuration can avoid.

The runtime-owned, coalesced direction described above matches what I found in practice.

I’ve been investigating this issue in the context of long-running training jobs. The practical failure mode is that a two-day training process may terminate after four hours, but the agent remains idle and the failure is not handled until the user returns much later.

I built and tested a proof of concept that:

 - wakes an idle thread when a background terminal or subagent completes;
 - queues the completion when a model turn or tool call is active, then injects it at the next safe boundary;
 - avoids polling and does not interrupt active model reasoning;
 - keeps terminal completion context bounded by line and token limits;
 - allows non-consuming tail inspection through write_stdin;
 - suppresses automatic wakeups for user-initiated termination.

 The implementation and comparison are available here:
 https://github.com/openai/codex/compare/main...ikerrrrrrrrrrr:codex:feature/runtime-wake-events

 This overlaps with the event-driven behavior proposed in #32188, while also covering late subagent completion.

The current PoC is rebased onto the latest main; the protocol suite passes 286/286 tests and the focused background-terminal suite passes 10/10 tests.
This branch is intended as a validated PoC rather than a proposal to review the entire 50-file diff as one PR.
Would the maintainers be interested in this architectural direction? If so, I’m happy to split the implementation into smaller reviewable stages and submit an invited PR.

One source-level refinement to the opt-in direction, based on current `main` at [`636e505c`](https://github.com/openai/codex/commit/636e505c5cd809bdce37314f77130ffb4e45c46b). This is design analysis, not a tested patch.

Normal Multi-Agent V2 spawn [explicitly skips the detached completion watcher](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/636e505c5cd809bdce37314f77130ffb4e45c46b/codex-rs/core/src/agent/control/spawn.rs#L571-L583). Its active terminal path is [`Session::maybe_notify_parent_of_terminal_turn`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/636e505c5cd809bdce37314f77130ffb4e45c46b/codex-rs/core/src/session/mod.rs#L1927-L1973) -> [`forward_child_completion_to_parent`](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/636e505c5cd809bdce37314f77130ffb4e45c46b/codex-rs/core/src/session/mod.rs#L1976-L2025), where `trigger_turn` is currently hard-coded to `false`. Therefore, changing the stale V2 branch in the detached watcher would not change the normal V2 completion path.

A backward-compatible per-spawn contract could be:

- `completion_delivery: queue_only | resume_parent`, defaulting to `queue_only`;
- persist the selected policy in `SubAgentSource::ThreadSpawn` (or equivalent durable spawn provenance), rather than only in ephemeral spawn arguments;
- let the active V2 terminal sink set `trigger_turn` only for `resume_parent`;
- route that mail through the existing pending-work scheduler, which already waits for an idle parent and drains trigger-turn mail together. This avoids adding another watcher, debounce timer, or parallel wake state machine.

The focused regression matrix would cover: default compatibility; one idle opt-in child producing one continuation; a busy parent deferring without interruption; two opt-in completions coalescing into one drain; restart preserving the policy; newer user work/cancellation winning; and nested completion targeting only the direct parent.

This would not replace an explicit all-of join. `resume_parent` is per-child completion delivery; a join remains the stronger contract when later work depends on an exact set of children and durable barrier receipts.

Would a backward-compatible per-spawn delivery option fit alongside the explicit join direction, and should its durable owner be `SubAgentSource::ThreadSpawn` or a separate runtime-owned registration, given that the collaboration tool schema is reserved?

The operational data point makes the acceptance boundary sharper: a wake primitive is ingress, not identity continuity and not authority.

I would test it as four receipts:

- delivery receipt: event id, stream id, cursor, attempt, and dedupe key are persisted before the wake can start a turn;
- target-resolution receipt: a stable role or subscription resolves to the current session, and a stale or missing target returns no_target rather than silently dropping;
- isolation receipt: two sessions on one host can subscribe to adjacent streams without either consuming, waking, or acknowledging the other's events;
- decision receipt: the delivered event is classified before side effects, with outcomes like wake, quarantine, drop_duplicate, defer, or require_approval.

Regression cases:

- an event lands during a reconnect gap and is replayed once after restart;
- the addressed session dies and a successor is resolved through a stable alias;
- the same event arrives twice and only one turn is created;
- a mid-turn burst is queued or coalesced according to the configured policy;
- a malformed event advances no cursor until it is quarantined or acknowledged as refused;
- a source authenticates but the event still cannot authorize work by itself.

The invariant I would keep is: external input can create a turn, but it cannot assign work, choose tools, or authorize side effects merely because it arrived from a configured source. Every wake should end with a typed outcome and an auditable cursor decision, including no_target and deduped cases, so the system never fails as a silent drop.

@hassaans Nice. We basically landed on the same design without comparing notes: a `monitor` tool running a watcher on top of `unified_exec`, every line it prints coming back as a notification, a flood guard for runaway output, and the watcher inheriting the session's sandbox so it reaches the same paths the turn already can.

I put a working version up a few comments back too: https://github.com/openai/codex/compare/rust-v0.142.0...yaanfpv:codex:monitor-tool. Mine's a bit further along if it's useful to pull from: it also runs in the desktop app, does persistent and one-shot watches, keeps a per-session registry for list/stop, and handles a few things that bit me along the way (trailing output racing the exit, the first slice of output before the watch attaches, and which live turn a watcher's output lands in when several are running). ~1,100 lines, behind a flag, no protocol change.

Either way, two of us building this independently probably says the design's about right. If the team would rather land one PR than two forks, I'll merge ours together.

Strong +1 for this feature. This is an important workflow gap compared with Claude Code's `Monitor` tool.

The key requirement is not just “run a command in the background”; Codex can already do that. The missing primitive is **event-driven wakeup**: let the agent start a watcher, become fully idle, and automatically resume only when the watcher emits an event or the background job changes state.

That matters for common coding workflows such as:

- waiting for a long build/test/training job to finish;
- watching logs for the first error or a specific pattern;
- reacting to file changes while working elsewhere;
- watching a dev server/process and resuming when it exits;
- waiting for CI/deployment state changes.

Today these workflows tend to require polling, which can trigger repeated model turns and replay a large context just to ask “is it done yet?”. An event-driven monitor would eliminate that idle token/turn overhead and make long-running agent workflows significantly more practical.

Ideal semantics are very close to what this proposal describes: `start / list / stop`, normal sandbox/approval rules, no model calls while quiet, and a watcher event waking the idle session into a new turn.

I would prioritize the core wakeup primitive over UI polish; once Codex can reliably wake an idle thread from a background event, richer monitoring UX can be built on top of it.

Adding a concrete user workflow where this missing primitive is currently a significant blocker.

I use coding agents for research / ML experimentation, where a typical workflow is not one long command but several independent long-running jobs managed concurrently from the same parent agent:

1. Launch experiments / evaluations A, B, and C in the background.
2. Let the parent turn finish and become genuinely idle — no model polling or monitoring subagent should be required while the jobs are simply running.
3. Whichever job completes first should emit a bounded completion event and wake the same parent thread exactly once.
4. The parent inspects the result, analyzes metrics/logs, decides what to do next, and may immediately launch experiment D or a corrected rerun.
5. The parent becomes idle again while the remaining jobs continue; each can independently trigger the same process later.

So for this workflow the missing `idle -> completion event -> continuation turn` transition is more than a token-efficiency issue. It prevents the agent from acting as an event-driven orchestrator for a dynamic experiment DAG. Today the practical choices are to keep the parent alive polling, dedicate a subagent to polling, or manually come back and prompt Codex after a job finishes. All three largely defeat the purpose of first-class background execution.

The behavior I would consider sufficient is essentially what this issue already proposes, with particular importance for multiple simultaneous outstanding jobs:

- opt-in completion wake per background task;
- zero model inference while all jobs are merely running;
- exactly-once wake on normal completion;
- queue/coalesce completion if another turn is active;
- bounded stdout/stderr + exit status/duration in the completion event;
- no duplicate wake if the completion was already consumed manually;
- cancellation/interrupt/session teardown must suppress stale wakes;
- several background jobs can remain independently tracked and wake the same parent over time.

This is currently one of the main reasons I use Claude Code instead of Codex for long-running research/experiment workflows: background Bash/subagents can complete asynchronously and notify/wake the main agent, which makes this kind of multi-experiment orchestration natural. Codex's model capabilities are otherwise well suited to the workflow; the blocker is the harness/runtime scheduling semantics.

Related end-to-end monitoring/orchestration request: #32993. For the narrower primitive, I think this issue (#32188) is the right canonical place rather than opening another duplicate.

